      "Chrysippus wrote several books on the Sorites (DL 7.192, 197). None survives. What we have is a sentence or two in the papyrus of his Logical Investigations (col. IX), a hostile paragraph in Cicero's Lucullus (Acad. 2.92 to 95), a sceptical use of the Stoic position in Sextus (M 7.416 to 421), a line in Plutarch that depends on an emendation (Comm. not. 1059d to e), and a formal premiss-and-conclusion Sorites that Diogenes reports in abridged form (DL 7.82). Williamson (Vagueness, 1994, ch. 1) read this material as the first epistemic theory of vagueness: every step of a Sorites expresses a proposition, every proposition is true or false, so there is a sharp cut-off that we cannot know, and the famous advice to fall silent is the confession of that ignorance. Bobzien (2002) answers that the inference from bivalence to epistemicism breaks at its second step, that the three pieces of independent evidence Williamson offers do not show what he needs, that silence was a move in a rule-governed game and not an epistemic report, and that if Chrysippus had anything like a theory of higher-order vagueness it was a theory of obscurity that is neutral between truth-value gaps and unknowable cut-offs. The session is hard for two reasons. The evidence is as thin as any in the course, so every claim must be sized to its locus, and Bobzien hedges her own reconstruction more than her conclusion suggests (n. 54 says the whole of section VI rests on an unproven supposition). The exercise builds her models 2 and 4 and shows what they do and do not settle.",
