          "Section VI separates two things Williamson's chapter runs together. Indefiniteness is semantic: a case is definite if true or false, indefinite if it has no truth value. Obscurity is epistemic: a case is obscure to us if it is not clear to us which of true, false, or indefinite it is. What is clear to us is as it seems; what is obscure to us may still be definitely true, false, or indefinite. For the question form Bobzien offers (p. 232): 'n grains make a heap' is obscure if and only if it is not clear whether to answer yes or no to 'do n grains make a heap?'. Iterating gives second-order obscurity (p. 233). N. 52 notes that this leaves open whether the obscurity comes from indefiniteness in the item or a limit in us. That neutrality is what the exercise exhibits.",
          "The models (p. 231; the diagram is transcribed in the corpus text). In odd-numbered models the series falls into sharply bounded areas and it is clear for every case which area it belongs to; in even-numbered models there lies between any two such areas a further area whose cases are obscure as to whether they belong left or right. Model 1: clear yes | clear no. Model 2: clear yes, an area obscure between clear yes and clear no, clear no. Model 3: clear yes | clearly obscure | clear no. Model 4: clear yes, an area obscure between clear yes and clearly obscure, clearly obscure, an area obscure between clearly obscure and clear no, clear no. N. 51 continues to models 5 and 6. The argument: Acad. 2.94 speaks of obscure cases, so model 1 is out; if the policy of stopping before the obscure means the Stoics thought we cannot know the first obscure case, then neither can we know the last clear one, so model 3 is out; Cicero's 'the obscure cases' suggests some cases are clearly obscure, which favours model 4 or higher over model 2. If that was the reason for stopping before the obscure, the advice handles every order of obscurity at once: stop before there is any tinge of it (p. 233). Everything after 'if' rests on the supposition n. 54 calls unproven, and the same note offers an alternative, reading (i) above, that needs no higher-order apparatus.",
