    briefing:
      "The final assessment is a 4,000-word paper and a viva. This session is about the paper: what standard it is held to, what topics are open enough to be worth a doctoral student's time and closed enough to be finished in a term, and how to write a proposal that an examiner who has read Bobzien 1996 can test before the paper exists. The standard is the one the course has used all along, now applied to your own writing rather than to Frede's or Williamson's. Every claim about a text carries a locus and an estimate of what that locus can bear. Every scholar is stated in a form the scholar would recognize. Every formal step is written out and checked, and where a result is relative to an encoding (as everything in the Themata laboratory is) the relativity is stated. Where the evidence is thin the sentence says so, in the main text and not in a footnote. The difficulty is that these standards pull against each other under a word limit: a paper that hedges every sentence says nothing, and a paper that says something must stake a claim the sources do not quite make. The skill is to put the one claim you are making in a form a reader can refute, to name what would refute it, and to spend the words on the evidence rather than the survey. Six topics follow with their sources and the trap in each. Each student brings a proposal in the stated format; the seminar reads it against the six quiz items, which are the Examiner's questions.",
